nts with sunny lines of slope 1. Similarly, slope -2 lines etc. So maybe we can cover all points with 4 sunny lines plus one vertical line. Let's try to find a covering: Use vertical line x=1 (non-sunny). Then we need to cover points:\n\n- Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\n- Column 3: (3,1),(3,2)\n- Column 4: (4,1)\n- Column 5: (5,1)\n\nTotal 7 points? Wait we counted incorrectly. Let's list all points for n=5:\n\na+b ≤ 6, a,b≥1.\n\nList by a:\n\na=1: b=1..5 => (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5)\na=2: b=1..4 => (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)\na=3: b=1..3 => (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)\na=4: b=1..2 => (4,1),(4,2)\na=5: b=1..1 => (5,1)\n\nTotal points: 5+4+3+2+1 = 15.\n\nNow vertical line x=1 covers (1,1)-(1,5). Remaining points: (2,1)-(2,4), (3,1)-(3,3), (4,1)-(4,2), (5,1). That's 10 points.\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines to cover these 10 points. Let's try to find a covering.\n\nPotential sunny lines:\n\n- L1: line through (2,1) and (3,2) and (4,3) (if (4,3) existed but it's not in set because (4,3) sum 7 >6). So L1 passes through (2,1) and (3,2). That's slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1. So line y = x - 1. It also passes through (4,3) which is not required, but that's okay. So L1 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Also (5,4) not required.\n\n- L2: line through (2,2) and (3,3) and (4,4) (not required). That's line y = x. It covers (2,2) and (3,3). Also (1,1) but already covered.\n\n- L3: line through (2,3) and (3,4) (not required) and (4,5) (not required). So line y = x + 1. It covers (2,3) only? Actually (2,3) sum 5, allowed. (3,4) sum 7 >6, not allowed. So L3 covers only (2,3). That's not efficient.\n\n- L4: line through (2,4) and (3,5) (not allowed). So L4 covers only (2,4).\n\n- L5: line through (3,1) and (4,2) and (5,3) (not allowed). That's line y = x - 2. It covers (3,1) and (4,2). (5,3) sum 8 >6, not allowed.\n\n- L6: line through (4,1) and (5,2) (not allowed). That's line y = x - 3. It covers (4,1) only.\n\n- L7: line through (5,1) and (4,2) (already covered by L5). That's line y = -x + 6? Actually (5,1) and (4,